## What happened

Apple has been deprecating DSA in its Security/CommonCrypto framework for years. In **.NET 11**, Microsoft completed the removal: `DSA.Create()` now throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS. It still works on Linux (via OpenSSL) and Windows (via CNG).

## How to fix it

**Short-term** — use OpenSSL on macOS so DSA remains available:

```csharp
// Install OpenSSL via Homebrew: brew install openssl
// Then load it explicitly on macOS:
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
using System.Security.Cryptography;

DSA dsa;
if (RuntimeInformation.IsOSPlatform(OSPlatform.OSX))
{
    // Use the OpenSSL-based implementation
    dsa = new DSAOpenSsl(); // from System.Security.Cryptography.OpenSsl
}
else
{
    dsa = DSA.Create();
}
```

**Long-term (recommended)** — migrate away from DSA entirely. DSA is a legacy algorithm with fixed 1024-bit key limits in FIPS 186-2. Replace with:

| Algorithm | Use case |
|-----------|----------|
| **ECDSA** | Drop-in modern signature replacement |
| **Ed25519** | If .NET 11's new API is available |
| **RSA** | If interop with older systems is needed |

If you only need to **verify** existing signatures (not create new ones) and can't migrate yet, the OpenSSL shim is the practical path. Wrap it behind an interface so the eventual migration to ECDSA is straightforward.
